# Deep Dive: Architecting Lock-Free LSM-B+Tree Engines: SIMD Vector Clocks, Page Recirculation, and Bounded WAL Shadowing

## Section 1: Executive Overview & The Abstraction Leak

Deploying high-throughput, transactional data storage onto resource-constrained edge gateways and mobile edge compute nodes exposes severe architectural failures in conventional database engines. Storage systems built on standard Log-Structured Merge-trees (LSM-trees) like RocksDB or embedded B+Tree engines like SQLite rely on OS-level abstractions that leak catastrophically under intermittent network connectivity and write-heavy workloads [1].

When edge nodes suffer network dropouts, state writes accumulate locally. Traditional LSM engines handle this burst via write-ahead logs (WAL) and memtables. However, as memtables fill, write-path stalling occurs when thread pools wait on background compaction locks [2]. Conversely, traditional B+Trees suffer extreme Write Amplification Factors (WAF) on NAND flash storage due to random 4 KiB page overwrites, causing severe flash translation layer (FTL) wear and I/O latency spikes.

Furthermore, state reconciliation across disconnected edge nodes requires deterministic multi-master conflict resolution. Standard vector clock implementations rely on dynamically allocated hash maps and heap-allocated string keys, introducing garbage collection pauses or heap fragmentation that breaks deterministic real-time execution bounds.

To bridge this abstraction gap, this treatise presents the architecture of a zero-allocation, lock-free LSM-B+Tree hybrid storage engine. By combining a lock-free lockless-skiplist memtable, memory-mapped page recirculation, SIMD-accelerated binary vector clocks, and shadow-ring-buffer WAL execution, we eliminate allocation pauses on the hot path and achieve deterministic sub-millisecond local commit latencies.

---
## Section 2: Low-Level Mechanics & Memory Layout

To achieve hardware-level throughput efficiency, data structures must respect CPU cache line architectures (64 bytes on modern x86_64 and ARM64 processors) and eliminate false sharing across concurrent CPU cores.

### 2.1 Struct Alignment & Cache Line Padding

The memtable node is structured as a variable-height skiplist node with lock-free atomic pointer forward links. Each skiplist node header is aligned to 64 bytes (`#[repr(align(64))]`) to enforce cache-line isolation between atomic write pointers and payload metadata.

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
|                      MemTable Node Header                         | 
|  +---------------------------+---------------------------------+  | 
|  | Lsn (u64, 8B)             | Vector Clock (128-bit, 16B)     |  | 
|  +---------------------------+---------------------------------+  | 
|  | Key Length (u16, 2B)      | Value Length (u32, 4B)          |  | 
|  +---------------------------+---------------------------------+  | 
|  | Tower Height (u16, 2B)    | Flags / Alignment Pad (32B)     |  | 
|  +---------------------------+---------------------------------+  | 
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
| Atomic Next Pointers Array [AtomicPtr<Node>; Tower Height]        | 
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
| Key Bytes (Variable) | Value Bytes (Variable)                     | 
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
```

### 2.2 Memory Alignment and Offset Layout

| Byte Offset | Field | Data Type | Alignment Requirement | Architectural Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `0x00 - 0x07` | `lsn` | `u64` | 8-byte | Log Sequence Number for atomic serialization |
| `0x08 - 0x17` | `vector_clock` | `u128` (`v128` SIMD) | 16-byte | SIMD 128-bit packed vector clock comparison |
| `0x18 - 0x19` | `key_len` | `u16` | 2-byte | Key size descriptor |
| `0x1A - 0x1D` | `val_len` | `u32` | 4-byte | Value size descriptor |
| `0x1E - 0x1F` | `height` | `u16` | 2-byte | Skiplist forward link height |
| `0x20 - 0x3F` | `_pad` | `[u8; 32]` | 32-byte | Cache line alignment padding (prevents false sharing) |
| `0x40 - Dynamic`| `next_ptrs` | `[AtomicPtr<Node>]` | 8-byte boundary | Lock-free CAS multi-level navigation links |

### 2.3 Memory-Mapped Page Recirculation Architecture

Instead of calling `malloc()` or kernel `brk()` allocations during WAL spools and SSTable (Sorted String Table) flushes, the storage engine preemptively maps raw physical memory pages using `mmap(2)` with `MAP_SHARED | MAP_POPULATE` and configures access hints via `madvise(MADV_WILLNEED)` [3].

Pages are divided into standard 4 KiB operating system frames arranged in a circular recirculation pool. Pages cycle through three strict state transitions managed via atomic state bitmasks: `FREE` -> `ACTIVE_WAL` -> `FROZEN_SST` -> `RECIRCULATING` -> `FREE`.

