## Section 5: Failure Modes & Chaos Engineering

In high-throughput, agentic runtimes, non-determinism is an ever-present vector of system degradation. Achieving deterministic replay requires anticipating non-deterministic state drift, thread contention, system call latency anomalies, and hardware-level instruction variations. Below, we formalize the operational failure modes of deterministic execution engines, detail their root causes, and provide actionable implementations for state verification, credit-based backpressure, time virtualization, and chaos testing.

```
