       |                       AGENT RUNTIME EXECUTION                         |
       +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
            |                                                      |
    [Syscall / vDSO Trap]                                 [Heap / Memory Access]
            |                                                      |
            v                                                      v
  +--------------------+                                 +--------------------+
  | Ring Buffer Log    |                                 | Merkle Tree Hash   |
  | (eBPF / io_uring)  |                                 | (State Differential|
  +--------------------+                                 +--------------------+
            |                                                      |
            +---------------------------+--------------------------+
                                        |
                                        v
                    +---------------------------------------+
                    |      DIVERGENCE VALIDATION ENGINE     |
                    +---------------------------------------+
                                        |
                  +---------------------+---------------------+
                  |                                           |
         [State Matched]                             [State Diverged]
                  |                                           |
                  v                                           v
     Continue Execution Epoch                         Raise `SIGTRAP`
                                                    Dump Core & Register Map
```

### 5.1 State Divergence Detection & Execution Register Dumps

State divergence occurs when the agent runtime’s register values, instruction pointers, or heap checksums deviate from the recorded log vector during execution replay. The most common vector is asynchronous signal delivery or uncaptured non-deterministic hardware flags (e.g., CPU `RDRAND` instructions or implicit floating-point rounding modes).

To mitigate unrecoverable state corruption, the replay supervisor executes a divergence check at every execution epoch boundary or system call trap. If a divergence is detected, the process freezes execution, issues a `SIGTRAP` signal, and serializes the hardware register file along with the current memory diff.

```c
// divergence_checker.c - Low-level register and state verification engine
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <sys/user.h>
#include <sys/ptrace.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

typedef struct {
    uint64_t rip;
    uint64_t rsp;
    uint64_t rbp;
    uint64_t rax;
    uint64_t rbx;
    uint64_t rcx;
    uint64_t rdx;
    uint32_t state_crc32;
} __attribute__((packed)) execution_snapshot_t;

typedef struct {
    uint64_t total_epochs;
    uint64_t divergence_events;
    bool enforce_strict_halt;
} replay_monitor_t;

void capture_register_context(pid_t child_pid, struct user_regs_struct *regs) {
    if (ptrace(PTRACE_GETREGS, child_pid, NULL, regs) < 0) {
        perror("[FATAL] Failed to fetch process register state via ptrace");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}

bool verify_execution_epoch(pid_t child_pid, const execution_snapshot_t *expected) {
    struct user_regs_struct actual_regs;
    capture_register_context(child_pid, &actual_regs);

    if (actual_regs.rip != expected->rip ||
        actual_regs.rsp != expected->rsp ||
        actual_regs.rax != expected->rax) {

        fprintf(stderr, "\n=======================================================\n");
        fprintf(stderr, "[CRITICAL] DIVERGENCE DETECTED AT PID %d\n", child_pid);
        fprintf(stderr, "EXPECTED RIP: 0x%016lx | ACTUAL RIP: 0x%016llx\n", expected->rip, actual_regs.rip);
        fprintf(stderr, "EXPECTED RSP: 0x%016lx | ACTUAL RSP: 0x%016llx\n", expected->rsp, actual_regs.rsp);
        fprintf(stderr, "EXPECTED RAX: 0x%016lx | ACTUAL RAX: 0x%016llx\n", expected->rax, actual_regs.rax);
        fprintf(stderr, "=======================================================\n");

        // Trigger SIGTRAP to preserve process core state for inspection
        ptrace(PTRACE_KILL, child_pid, NULL, NULL);
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

### 5.2 Credit-Based Write-Ahead Log (WAL) Backpressure

When the system operates under heavy external event ingress, the Write-Ahead Log (WAL) ring buffer can become saturated if disk I/O latency spikes. Allowing the agent to execute while the WAL ring buffer is full results in unrecorded syscalls, violating replay invariants.

We enforce a credit-based backpressure policy at the kernel-user boundary using atomic credit registers shared via Linux `mmap`. The agent must acquire execution credits prior to entering ring-0 syscall paths.

| Operational State | Ring Capacity Utilized | Credit Allocation Action | System Impact |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Nominal Ingress** | `< 60%` | Maximum credit allotment (`CREDIT_MAX = 1024`) | Unthrottled runtime execution; sub-millisecond logging latency. |
| **High Contention** | `60% - 85%` | Linear decay dynamic throttling | Ingress rate scaled down; non-critical telemetry streams dropped. |
| **WAL Saturation** | `> 85%` | Hard credit exhaustion (`CREDIT_AVAIL = 0`) | `sys_enter` hooks block agent threads via `futex` wait until flush. |
| **I/O Failure** | `100% (Overflow)` | Emergency signal emission (`SIGILL` / Hard Pause) | Immediate state lock; runtime memory frozen to prevent state corruption. |

```rust
// wal_backpressure.rs - Atomic credit-based flow control module
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;

const MAX_CREDITS: u64 = 1024;
const CRITICAL_WAL_THRESHOLD: u64 = 870; // 85% of 1024 ring slots

pub struct CreditController {
    available_credits: Arc<AtomicU64>,
    ring_tail_index: Arc<AtomicU64>,
    ring_head_index: Arc<AtomicU64>,
}

impl CreditController {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            available_credits: Arc::new(AtomicU64::new(MAX_CREDITS)),
            ring_tail_index: Arc::new(AtomicU64::new(0)),
            ring_head_index: Arc::new(AtomicU64::new(0)),
        }
    }

    #[inline(always)]
    pub fn acquire_execution_credit(&self) -> Result<(), &'static str> {
        loop {
            let current_credits = self.available_credits.load(Ordering::Acquire);
            if current_credits == 0 {
                // Yield execution context to prevent CPU spinning while WAL flushes
                thread::sleep(Duration::from_micros(50));
                continue;
            }

            if self.available_credits.compare_exchange_weak(
                current_credits,
                current_credits - 1,
                Ordering::AcqRel,
                Ordering::Relaxed,
            ).is_ok() {
                return Ok(());
            }
        }
    }

    pub fn reconcile_wal_flush(&self, flushed_slots: u64) {
        let tail = self.ring_tail_index.fetch_add(flushed_slots, Ordering::SeqCst);
        let head = self.ring_head_index.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
        let current_occupancy = head.saturating_sub(tail);

        if current_occupancy < CRITICAL_WAL_THRESHOLD {
            // Restore credits proportional to freed slots
            self.available_credits.fetch_add(flushed_slots, Ordering::Release);
        }
    }
}
```

### 5.3 Clock Skew Virtualization via vDSO Hooking

To prevent agentic runtimes from detecting time anomalies during deterministic replay, standard system clocks (`clock_gettime`, `gettimeofday`) must be fully virtualized. Modern Linux platforms optimize timestamp fetching via Virtual Dynamic Shared Object (`vDSO`), which bypasses standard system call trap mechanisms and executes in user space. 

To intercept `vDSO` time calls without re-compiling runtime binaries, the infrastructure uses ELF binary function patching via `LD_PRELOAD` or dynamic instrumentation to detour `vDSO` entry points to a virtualized dynamic clock counter.

```c
// vdso_time_shim.c - Dynamic interception of virtualized clock calls
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef int (*real_clock_gettime_t)(clockid_t, struct timespec *);
static real_clock_gettime_t real_clock_gettime_func = NULL;

// Monotonically advancing internal clock ticks for deterministic timing
static uint64_t g_virtual_nanoseconds = 1700000000000000000ULL;
static const uint64_t TIME_STEP_PER_CALL = 1000000ULL; // 1ms invariant step

int clock_gettime(clockid_t clk_id, struct timespec *tp) {
    if (!tp) return -1;

    // Intercept Monotonic and Realtime clocks to ensure replay determinism
    if (clk_id == CLOCK_REALTIME || clk_id == CLOCK_MONOTONIC) {
        g_virtual_nanoseconds += TIME_STEP_PER_CALL;
        tp->tv_sec = g_virtual_nanoseconds / 1000000000ULL;
        tp->tv_nsec = g_virtual_nanoseconds % 1000000000ULL;
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback to true dynamic loading if unhandled clock type requested
    if (!real_clock_gettime_func) {
        real_clock_gettime_func = (real_clock_gettime_t)dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "clock_gettime");
    }
    return real_clock_gettime_func(clk_id, tp);
}
```

### 5.4 Controlled Chaos Engineering for Replay Validation

To ensure the durability of the replay pipeline, chaos engineering fault injection must be executed against the underlying system abstractions. We introduce chaos hooks at three distinct system levels:

1. **System Call Injection:** Injecting random `EAGAIN`, `EINTR`, or `ETIMEDOUT` error codes into `sys_enter` ring buffers to test agent recovery routines.
2. **Scheduler Permutation:** Forcing thread context switches at random instruction boundaries using `PTRACE_SINGLESTEP` to ensure race conditions do not leak state.
3. **Log Corruption Interception:** Injecting bit-flips into recorded state frames to confirm that the integrity-checking vector (e.g., CRC32/SHA-256 state Merkle trees) halts execution rather than proceeding with corrupted inputs.

