# Architecting Disaggregated GPU Memory Fabrics

## 1. Architectural Motivation & Scalability Cliff
As LLM parameter counts scale, the KV-cache memory requirement grows linearly with sequence length and batch size. In monolithic serving, the VRAM capacity of a single GPU (or node) acts as a hard ceiling for concurrent request throughput. This "Memory Wall" forces engineers to over-provision GPU compute simply to gain access to HBM capacity. Disaggregation decouples compute from memory, allowing the KV-cache to reside in a remote, high-bandwidth memory fabric, effectively treating the network as a cache-coherent extension of the local GPU memory space.

